gm-method FIX (follows the cpu-gpu-fractal-height-float-precision-divergence diagnosis): a CPU height sampler (JS transpile of a GLSL fractal) that floats/sinks vs the GPU-rendered surface by metres is float64-vs-float32 accumulation. THE FIX that worked: in the JS GLSL-runtime's op chokepoint (_bin/_u that back add/sub/mul/div + unary), wrap each SCALAR result in Math.fround so the JS fractal accumulates in float32 like the GPU. RESULT: CPU-vs-GPU height gap ~2.5m -> ~0.06-0.74m (live-witnessed via stabilized sampleGroundM). CRITICAL TUNING LESSON: fround ONLY the per-op _bin/_u results - do NOT also fround the dot-product/normalize/mat3mul accumulators: that OVERSHOT (gap got WORSE, 0.74m->3.1m) because the GPU uses wider intermediates/FMA for dot products. Per-op fround is a close HEURISTIC, never bit-exact across GPU vendors/FMA, so a sub-metre residual remains - acceptable for collision; exact 0 needs a shared baked heightfield (GPU bakes, CPU reads). LANDING checklist (multi-repo): (1) edit the SDK glsl-rt _bin/_u; (2) the emitted transpile (gen-height) is UNCHANGED (fround is runtime, not emitted) - only LF/CRLF in the regen diff; (3) the golden height-sample test WILL fail - RE-BLESS it to the new float32 values (matching the GPU IS the goal, this is intended churn) and relax any geometry colinearity/tolerance assert from 1e-9 to ~1e-4 (f32 epsilon); (4) consumer bridges it via a postinstall patch (idempotent _fr/_bin/_u injections) verified to apply to a FRESH npm-installed SDK so the deploy path gets it, until the SDK republishes. WITNESS: the collider/veg debug-draw wireframe hugs the rendered terrain instead of floating; numeric gap drops. NEVER change the visible geometry - the GPU stays f32, you match the CPU TO it.